WEBVTT

question-0
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.250 line:78% size:92%
Global placement leaves fractional coordinates. Moving cells to the nearest sites and rows looks like the last step. Is it
really?

question-1
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:21.983 line:78% size:92%
Look at these six cells. After snapping, all align, but A and B choose the same location and C intersects them too. Three
overlapping pairs remain.

site-0
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:32.783 line:78% size:92%
The site width is one. A has x equal to three point seven, so its nearest integer is four. B at four point one also chooses
four.

site-1
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:42.183 line:78% size:92%
Every cell makes its decision independently. The rounding rule never asks whether another cell has already claimed
that location.

row-0
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:53.450 line:78% size:92%
Allowed row bottoms are zero, two and four. A at y equal to one point two is point eight from row two, closer than one
point two from row zero.

row-1
00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:04.983 line:78% size:92%
The y coordinate is the lower-left corner, not the center. Cell height equals row height two, so the row bottom plus the
full height must remain inside the chip.

collision-0
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:16.817 line:78% size:92%
A and B both finish at four, two, while C finishes at five, two. Each has width two, so A/B, A/C and B/C all overlap.

collision-1
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:29.617 line:78% size:92%
This is the central limitation of greedy snap: it quantizes coordinates only. Perfect site and row alignment does not
imply non-overlapping placement.

clamp-0
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:41.517 line:78% size:92%
After rounding, clamp x between zero and W minus w. On a width-six chip, a width-two cell can place its lower-left
corner no farther right than four.

clamp-1
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:51.383 line:78% size:92%
Checking x against the chip width alone is insufficient. A width-two cell whose lower-left corner is at five already
extends beyond six.

fixed-0
00:01:51.433 --> 00:02:03.083 line:78% size:92%
The teacher data locks D at eight, four, although the float draft records eight point four, three point eight. The
algorithm restores D to its constraint position.

fixed-1
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:15.417 line:78% size:92%
This does not optimize a fixed macro. Restoring the constraint creates a coordinate difference of point six, which must
be explicitly included or excluded from displacement.

displacement-0
00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:26.750 line:78% size:92%
Summing Manhattan distances between lower-left corners gives all-cell L1 displacement four point four. Fixed D’s
constraint restoration contributes point six.

displacement-1
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:36.217 line:78% size:92%
Counting only movable cells gives three point eight. Both quantities can be reported, but you must state which cells
were counted.

hpwl-0
00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:48.383 line:78% size:92%
HPWL uses cell centers: x plus half the width, and y plus half the row height. Counting each of six nets once gives total
HPWL thirty-two.

hpwl-1
00:02:48.433 --> 00:03:01.650 line:78% size:92%
HPWL measures connectivity, not overlap. An attractive metric cannot replace site, row, bounds, fixed-constraint and
non-overlap checks.

ties-0
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:13.117 line:78% size:92%
Python uses ties-to-even: two point five becomes two, and three point five becomes four. JavaScript’s Math.round
gives three for two point five.

ties-1
00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:25.717 line:78% size:92%
This site explicitly uses ties-to-even in both languages. Equal row distances choose the first listed row. Random tests do
not replace exact boundary cases.

touching-0
00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:35.317 line:78% size:92%
Place width-one A at x equal to one and width-one B at two. Their edges touch, but they share no positive area.

touching-1
00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:45.717 line:78% size:92%
Use strict inequalities for interval intersection. Treating contact as overlap would wrongly reject valid adjacent sites.

limits-0
00:03:45.767 --> 00:04:01.183 line:78% size:92%
Removing collisions requires later row packing, overlap removal or a method such as Abacus. This lesson explains
greedy snap without secretly adding another solver.

limits-1
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:12.917 line:78% size:92%
Inspect every failed check before choosing the next step. An aligned placement that still overlaps is a useful
counterexample, not a successful legalization result.

try-it-0
00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:25.283 line:78% size:92%
Edit a fractional coordinate in the lab, inspect its target step by step, then try halfway values, boundary clamps and
edge contact. Learn the rule, not just the button.

try-it-1
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:36.983 line:78% size:92%
Finally, recompute the same data in real Python. Alignment, displacement, wirelength and legality are four questions
that must be explained separately.
